## B2 — PASS

Claim: Cap 1048576 in collect_tokens and tokenize_lines. Overflow prints ERR lex too many tokens and process_exit(1).

Proof
- ooda/oodac/token_stream.oo:22-24 collect_tokens: if list_len(out) >= 1048576 { println("ERR\tlex\ttoo many tokens"); process_exit(1); }
- ooda/oodac/token_fmt.oo:25-28 tokenize_lines: after collect_tokens, if list_len(toks) > 1048576 { same ERR; process_exit(1); }

Same number, not the same rail (chars, not tokens)
- ooda/oodac/lex.oo:16-17 lex_all: if chars_len(src) > 1048576 { return Err("source exceeds 1MiB limit"); } No process_exit. Caller tokenize_lines:17-23 exits on that Err.
- ooda/oodac/check_mod.oo:16-18: chars_len(body) > 1048576 → ERR check source exceeds 1MiB expanded limit + process_exit(1).
- ooda/oodac/check_drive.oo:30-32: same char cap + same ERR text as check_mod.

Holes (claim can [x] while product is still wrong)
1. tokenize_lines:26 `>` is dead. collect_tokens already process_exit on `>=` before it can return more than 1048576 rows. The wrap cap is theater.
2. Off-by-one: collect_tokens may return 1048576 (last push is EOF) and tokenize_lines accepts it. A 1048576-char all-punct file (lex_all allows n==1048576) then dies on the extra EOF push. lex says OK; token cap says ERR.
3. Cap is per tokenize_lines call, not process life. np>12 check_mod tokenizes each body alone. Many 1MiB modules do not trip "too many tokens".
4. Count is not RSS. list_push still copies. 1M token rows + source string can still blow heap before the if. dump_tokens (token_dump.oo:12-58) walks lex_step with no token-count cap (only lex_all's 1MiB char cap).
5. No fixture string "too many tokens" under ooda/fixtures. No product smoke that the ERR fires.
6. Double walk: tokenize_lines lex_all then collect_tokens. Cap does not cut that cost.

